let's start with the Roger Scruton  affair because it's something that's  quite recently something you've written  about you were heavily involved in in  fact for anyone who didn't follow it  just tell us what happened  oh yeah the long and short of it is that  the very distinguished British  philosopher Roger Scruton now in his  mid-70s author of 40 books on a range of  subjects aesthetics philosophy  architecture music gave an interview to  a magazine in the UK called the New  Statesman and the deputy editor of the  New Statesman one George Eaton  misrepresented what had been said in the  interview a lot about what had been said  in the interview and caused wider  Scruton to be fired within five hours  from the position he held a non-stop  ndrine on an unpaid position advising a  British government crane go and this may  seem all like whereas where's a lot of  people people not familiar with Scruton  or government quangos may think well so  what so what is that I hold this very  old-fashioned view which is you  shouldn't be allowed to just lie about  people and get away with it and this was  one of the most blatant flagrant  examples of somebody doing that in  journalism it can happen in other fields  trades as well but one of the most  flavorant examples I've seen in my life  in journalism is somebody doing that and  I knew from the moment I read the  alleged quotes that they were untrue  because I've been fortunate enough to  know Roger Scruton for almost 20 years  now and I know that he doesn't think of  the Chinese a people made up of weird  robot automate ins or something I knew  that it was wrong that this this hack  had said this I looked into it sure  enough but I just guten hadn't said that  he'd said that the Chinese Communist  Party is trying to make the Chinese  people into automaton so that's just a  lie and we are all I think sadly for all  sorts of political and other reasons  becoming used to a low standard of truth  in public as in private life but  sometimes that comes right up to your  door and you just got to stop it so I  worked quite hard  recent weeks to expose this scandal and  to try to reverse it and although Roger  Scruton remains sacked from that  government role it's now known and  recognized widely that the new statesman  is deputy editor Georgie too lied  misrepresented the contents of the  interview and that this was dishonest  journalism at its worst and so um so yes  I am we turned it around a bit now one  of the things in this affair that struck  me particularly was that I think the  journalists in question had tweeted or  put something on his Instagram when he  has a picture of himself  swinging champagne for having achieved  to firing up nearly by lying about them  yeah this is particularly ugly thing to  do after after getting somebody fired  from their job he had he had George  Eaton had posted this photograph on  Instagram subsequently I removed it but  of him swinging champagnes saying the  feeling you get when you get racists and  homophobes Scruton fired from his  government position by the way the the  homophobe claim was particularly  interesting because the tape of the  interview which I subsequently acquired  showed that George Eaton himself said  something which by his own lights would  be homophobic the question he put to  Roger Scruton was you've been criticized  in the past for saying that  homosexuality is not normal but that's  just a statement of the obvious that's  what George Eaton says Roger Scruton  then says oh well no when I say not  normal normal I mean it's not the norm  in the same way that having ginger hair  isn't the norm so this journalist says  something that is homophobic and then it  tributes the homophobia to the person  who didn't say something homophobic  who's the target of his smear campaign  so it's a really interesting lesson I  think this happens sometimes in  journalism there are times when you can  just see how the whole wretched hit job  is done and that's what I wanted to do  that's why I wrote  cover piece in The Spectator a few weeks  ago now called the anatomy of a hit job  because you know I'm fortunate enough in  this position because I knew the person  who the accusations were being made  about but I mean I've been overwhelmed  by messages since but as you can imagine  by more people than usual saying look  what's happened to me and I've this has  been said about me and so on and so in a  way I think it's resonated with a  slightly wider number of people and  wouldn't normally be the case because I  think we are all we've all been becoming  familiar in recent years with the fact  that the media is not honest with very  basic thing I'm not saying all the media  I'm not saying all journalists have some  fine journalists but as as the model of  funding in journalism dies or at least  stumbles very severely it's sort of  hollowed out and I say this as a  journalist myself it's being hollowed  out and in the space of reporting and  having to really stick very carefully  facts there's been this melding  something's been happening whereby an  activist like this guy George Eaton can  go in to try to take out an interview  subject the aim of the interview is  therefore not an interview it's to do  something else and I think that a lot of  people have noticed in recent years a  tendency in television and in print  journalism to move from doing the thing  that you hold yourself out as doing and  to do something else and once you work  out what that something else is it's  very apart from the other scales for  heisman because after that people don't  trust the media again  you  [Music]
 

